  "prediction": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\n**Wells Fargo Scandal: Sen. Elizabeth Warren Calls for Resignation and Criminal Investigation**\n\nSen. Elizabeth Warren (D-Mass) grilled Wells Fargo CEO John Stumpf during a Senate hearing, calling for his resignation and a criminal investigation into the bank's executives. The hearing was held in response to the bank's cross-selling scandal, which involved creating millions of fake accounts for customers without their knowledge. Warren accused Stumpf of \"gutless leadership\" and said that the only way to reform the industry is to hold executives accountable by sending them to jail. The senator also criticized Stumpf's compensation package, which included a performance bonus of $12.5 million in 2015. Wells Fargo has agreed to pay $185 million in penalties for the scandal.\n\n**Middle East Politics: Trump to Recognize Jerusalem as Israel's Capital**\n\nUS President Donald Trump is expected to announce that he plans to recognize Jerusalem as Israel's capital on Wednesday. The decision is seen as a compromise, as Trump had previously pledged to move the US embassy to Jerusalem, which would have sparked an even greater international backlash. However, the administration has insisted that it still plans to move the embassy to Jerusalem, but will sign a six-month waiver to delay the move. The decision has been met with criticism from world leaders, including Pope Francis, who called on the US to respect the status quo in Jerusalem. The Palestinian representative to the UK, Manuel Hassassian, described the recognition of Jerusalem as Israel's capital as the \"kiss of death\" to the peace process.\n\n**Saudi Arabia's Peace Plan: A Shift in Middle East Politics**\n\nThe New York Times reported on Sunday that Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman presented a peace plan to Palestinian leader Mahmoud Abbas that would give the Palestinians a state with limited sovereignty and noncontiguous parts of the West Bank. The plan would also allow most Israeli settlements in the West Bank to remain and would not grant the Palestinians the right of return for refugees and their descendants. The Saudis have denied the contents of the report, but the move is seen as a shift in Middle East politics, with both Saudi Arabia and the US growing closer to Israel. The move is likely to further complicate the Israeli-Palestinian peace process.",
